{:edb/db-before-t 2678 :edb/db-after-t 2679 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535991 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:26:21.620Z" 13194139535991 true] [17592186228015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378504 13194139535991 true] [17592186229114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378505 13194139535991 true] [17592186229114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378508 13194139535991 true] [17592186229114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378512 13194139535991 true] [17592186229115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378516 13194139535991 true] [17592186229115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378523 13194139535991 true] [17592186229116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378519 13194139535991 true] [17592186229116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378539 13194139535991 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378528 13194139535991 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378531 13194139535991 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378535 13194139535991 true] [17592186229582 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186229582 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535991 false] [17592186229582 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 3y = 6e^{-3x}$,\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n$y_p(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 false] [17592186229582 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186229582 1145 "The forcing exponential $e^{-3x}$ already occurs in the complementary solution, so use $y_p=Bxe^{-3x}$. Its derivative is $B(1-3x)e^{-3x}$. Thus $y_p^{\\prime}+3y_p=Be^{-3x}$. Comparing with $6e^{-3x}$ gives $B=6$, hence $y_p(x)=6xe^{-3x}$." 13194139535991 false] [17592186229582 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186229589 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186229589 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535991 false] [17592186229589 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } - y = 2e^{2x}$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 false] [17592186229589 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186229589 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186229589 1145 "Try $y_p=Be^{2x}$. Substitution gives $y_p^{\\prime}-y_p=(2B-B)e^{2x}=Be^{2x}$, so $B=2$. Add the complementary solution to obtain $y(x)=Ae^x+2e^{2x}$." 13194139535991 false] [17592186301947 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378511 13194139535991 true] [17592186301970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378527 13194139535991 true] [17592186378504 1034 "6e^{-3x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378504 1111 #uuid "7d54c2b7-2fd4-5ac6-9b17-e3e1e75fe048" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378506 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1089 "q-332644" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1113 #uuid "8903add6-0b71-512c-a68b-c77444eff598" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378505 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378507 13194139535991 true] [17592186378506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535991 true] [17592186378506 1041 "field-1" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378507 13194139535991 true] [17592186378506 1110 #uuid "b442ff4e-d61d-572e-aa1f-a0e72d9b120b" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378507 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}e^{4x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378507 1111 #uuid "99e462e1-4c24-599b-9784-bc6a819f34bb" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378509 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1089 "q-332659" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-18y=24{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1113 #uuid "07b0d75a-b632-5a31-b7e2-32353413f5df" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378508 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $24{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})-18(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-12-18)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -30\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378510 13194139535991 true] [17592186378509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535991 true] [17592186378509 1041 "field-1" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378510 13194139535991 true] [17592186378509 1110 #uuid "f8f497ed-1344-5460-9b0a-6f4b2b8a164a" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378510 1034 "-\\frac{4}{5}e^{-2x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378510 1111 #uuid "56622ace-6fee-5f7e-9817-a3fb4b9d29eb" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378511 1034 "\\frac{7}{12}e^{x}-\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378511 1111 #uuid "0086491b-8f28-5d3f-b14a-87b1827fd9ab" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378513 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1089 "q-332669" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1113 #uuid "aba6e3a2-6b9c-5da4-a0fb-f7b0bbffa2ed" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378512 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378514 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378515 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1041 "field-1" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378515 13194139535991 true] [17592186378513 1110 #uuid "313afa76-69ee-5070-9624-01f055352dba" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378514 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}-\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378514 1111 #uuid "8e49c025-3cae-5a35-a78c-63f2aa273051" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378515 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378515 1111 #uuid "332865e6-9a5b-5d5c-b86b-117ec5da9654" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378517 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1089 "q-332686" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1113 #uuid "9b63cbf2-f8fc-50ae-8f0d-7e9e3508d562" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378516 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+4\\alpha x{e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378518 13194139535991 true] [17592186378517 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535991 true] [17592186378517 1041 "field-1" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378517 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378518 13194139535991 true] [17592186378517 1110 #uuid "a57619ce-03f2-5496-8140-e8c0883afbc0" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378518 1034 "8xe^{-4x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378518 1111 #uuid "0b39559e-dadc-5abc-a8e3-a5022294e4a2" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378520 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1089 "q-332801" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=20{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1113 #uuid "cb257cc1-ec43-5317-b99b-94cdb5d3aa8c" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378519 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $20{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378521 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378522 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1041 "field-1" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378521 13194139535991 true] [17592186378520 1110 #uuid "690db9fd-5d56-5bdb-8177-c16979aaa76a" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378521 1034 "Ae^{x}+5e^{5x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535991 true] [17592186378521 1111 #uuid "cee59641-63fe-5422-ac53-afc2d3bf4ffc" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378522 1034 "Ae^{x}-5e^{5x}" 13194139535991 true] [17592186378522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131941395359